Two saved projections and their common commutant
================================================

Purpose
-------
This is a conditional alternative to closing either end group.  The two
accepted tensor applications already supply positive congruences for H and R.
The new calculation checks a common candidate form Q, converts the two saved
root tensors to self-adjoint projections, and measures their common commutant.
Neither its dimension nor the proposed algebra relations are presumed.
The independent conditional lemma is in theory.tex.  Its elementary quotient
and adjoint calculation identifies forty-four norm-one units in either of the
specified algebras, and twenty-two of determinant one in dimension seven.

The successful diagnostic is only a certificate for that conditional lemma.
It awards no IP, generation, saved-row, job, class, manuscript or conjecture
credit.  If the measured dimension is larger, the basis is dependent, or a
relation fails, the result is inconclusive and the measured matrices, RREF,
minor indices and inverse products are retained in proof.json.  A malformed
input or an unclosed input bank cannot produce a supported certificate.

Borrowed premises and new work
-----------------------------
The accepted old local journal has all twenty-two distinct middle elements.
Its kernel.py uses r[pair]*r[pair+1] for each rotation; for the middle pair this
is r1*r2.  The new caller reads its rotation events one and two, and reflection
event zero, as U, U squared and B.  It verifies B equals Original r1.  It does
not rebuild the old dihedral group or multiply U by itself again.

The two tensor roots are read from their exact saved proof fields.  Their
previous positive congruences, span invariance, natural frame, named-group
membership and old local-IP result are qualified borrowed premises.  The two
historic selector descriptors need not coincide with the fresh eligibility
selector: identities and operands are joined separately and all old editions
remain pinned.  No old tensor orbit, span, middle closure, end group or
projective action is repeated.

The fresh work checks Q symmetric and invertible, all four marks Q-isometric
and of determinant one, both projector equations and ranks four and three,
and the full four-plus-three diagonalization.  It then builds the forty-nine
commutation equations in twenty-five block entries.  A complete inverse of a
selected minor proves an upper dimension bound.  Independent candidate
matrices which commute with both projections give the matching lower bound.
The N adjoint, anticommutation, nilpotence, basis and exponential equations
are all direct tests.  Both dimensions four and six are supported.  No unit
group, group orbit or end order is enumerated or inferred.

